## Explanation
The `fork()` system call is a way for a running program to create a "clone" of itself. Imagine you are working on a complex document, and you decide to create an exact copy of that document so you can work on two different sections simultaneously without losing your place in the original. In C, when a program calls `fork()`, the operating system creates a new process (a separate instance of your program) that is an identical twin of the original. The original is called the "parent," and the new copy is called the "child." Both processes continue running from the exact same line of code, but they have their own separate memories and variables to work with.

## Use Cases
`fork()` is primarily used in systems programming to perform multitasking. For example, a web server might use `fork()` to create a new child process every time a user visits a website; the child handles that specific user's request, while the parent stays free to listen for new incoming visitors. It is also used in command-line shells (like the terminal) to launch new programs; the shell "forks" itself to create a copy, and then that copy transforms into the program you want to run.

